A person's eyes are at point $P$, height $h$ above the surface of a planet, modelled as a sphere of radius $R$ with centre $O$; the person's feet are at $N$, directly below $P$ on the surface. Line $PC$ is a tangent to the surface at the horizon point $C$. (Diagram not to scale.)
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (0,3.5);
\coordinate (F) at (0,-2.5);
\coordinate (N) at (0,2.5);
\coordinate (C) at (1.7496,1.7857);
\draw (O) circle (2.5);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\draw (P) -- (F);
\draw (P) -- (C);
\fill (N) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above] at (P) {$P$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[left] at (N) {$N$};
\node[midway, right] at ($(P)!0.5!(N)$) {$h$};
\coordinate (co) at ($(C)!0.25cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (cp) at ($(C)!0.25cm!(P)$);
\draw (co) -- ($(co)+(cp)-(C)$) -- (cp);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Show that the distance $d = PC$ to the horizon satisfies $d^{2} = h(2R + h)$.
(b) Hence find $d$, to the nearest metre, for a person of height $h = 2\text{ m}$ standing on a planet of radius $R = 6000\text{ km}$.
